+++
title: Alexandria
description: City in Egypt
hook: Many of the most interesting parts of Hellenistic Alexandria are under
  water. Cleopatra VII's royal quarters were inundated by earthquakes and
  tsunami, and a French underwater archaeologist has been investigating the
  sunken palace quarter since 1992.
standfirst: Built near an older Egyptian settlement called Rhacotis,
  Alexandria became capital of the Ptolemaic Kingdom and, for much of the
  Hellenistic age and late antiquity, the foremost commercial, intellectual
  and cultural centre. At one time it was the most populous city in the
  ancient world, and it held the largest library in it.
key-facts:
  - name: Founded
    value: 331 BC, by Alexander the Great
  - name: Where
    value: Alexandria Governorate, Egypt, on the Mediterranean coast
  - name: People
    value: 5,362,517 in the urban area in 2023 — Egypt's 2nd largest city
  - name: Metropolitan area
    value: 6,100,000 people over 1,661 km2 (641 sq mi)
  - name: Height above the sea
    value: −1 m (−3.3 ft)
  - name: Governor
    value: Ayman Mohammed Ibrahim Ahmed Ateyya
pull-quotes:
  - text: a trading market for all nations
    attribution: Benjamin of Tudela, the Jewish traveller
    section: islamic-era
timeline:
  - when: April 331 BC
    what: Alexander the Great founds Alexandria, on the site of Rhacotis.
    from: Alexandria was founded by [[ Alexander the Great ]] in April 331 BC as
      Ἀλεξάνδρεια (//Alexandreia//), as [[ one of his many city foundations |
      list-of-cities-founded-by-alexander-the-great ]].
  - when: 323 BC
    what: Alexander dies; his general Ptolemy Lagides takes Egypt and brings
      Alexander's body with him.
    from: Following Alexander's death in 323 BC, his general [[ Ptolemy Lagides |
      ptolemy-i-soter ]] took possession of Egypt and brought Alexander's body
      to Egypt with him.[[ ^18 ]]
  - when: 322/321 BC
    what: Ptolemy has Cleomenes, who had overseen the city's early development,
      executed.
    from: In 322/321 BC he had Cleomenes executed.
  - when: 305 BC
    what: Ptolemy declares himself Pharaoh as Ptolemy I Soter and moves his
      capital to Alexandria.
    from: Finally, in 305 BC, Ptolemy declared himself [[ Pharaoh ]] as Ptolemy I
      Soter ("Savior") and moved his capital to Alexandria.
  - when: 47 BC
    what: The Library faces destruction during Caesar's siege of the city.
    from: ([[ Library of Alexandria ]], which faced destruction during [[ Caesar |
      julius-caesar ]]'s [[ siege of Alexandria | siege-of-alexandria-47-bc ]]
      in 47 BC)
  - when: 38 AD
    what: Disturbances between Jews and Greek citizens — the Alexandrian pogroms —
      break out during a visit by King Agrippa I, according to Philo of
      Alexandria.
    from: According to [[ Philo of Alexandria ]], in the year 38 AD, disturbances
      erupted between Jews and Greek citizens of Alexandria during a visit
      paid by King [[ Agrippa I ]] to Alexandria, principally over the respect
      paid by the Herodian nation to the [[ Roman emperor ]], which quickly
      escalated to open affronts and violence between the two ethnic groups
      and the desecration of Alexandrian synagogues. This event has been
      called the [[ Alexandrian pogroms | alexandrian-riots-38 ]].
  - when: 115 AD
    what: Large parts of the city are destroyed in the Diaspora revolt; Hadrian
      and his architect Decriannus rebuild it.
    from: In 115 AD, large parts of Alexandria were destroyed during the [[
      Diaspora revolt ]], which gave [[ Hadrian ]] and his architect, [[
      Decriannus ]], an opportunity to rebuild it.
  - when: 215 AD
    what: Caracalla, insulted by the city's satires, orders his troops to kill all
      youths capable of bearing arms.
    from: In 215 AD, the emperor [[ Caracalla ]] visited the city and, because of
      some insulting [[ satires | satire ]] that the inhabitants had directed
      at him, abruptly commanded his troops to put to death all youths capable
      of bearing arms.
  - when: 21 July 365 AD
    what: A tsunami devastates Alexandria, commemorated annually as a "day of
      horror".
    from: On 21 July 365 AD, Alexandria was devastated by a [[ tsunami ]] ([[ 365
      Crete earthquake ]]),[[ ^24 ]] an event annually commemorated years
      later as a "day of horror".[[ ^25 ]]
  - when: AD 451
    what: After the Council of Chalcedon the Alexandrian Church splits between the
      Miaphysites and the Melkites.
    from: After the [[ Council of Chalcedon ]] in AD 451, the Alexandrian Church
      split between the [[ Miaphysites | miaphysitism ]] and the [[ Melkites |
      melkite ]].
  - when: "619"
    what: Alexandria falls to the Sassanid Persians; a new palace, later called
      "fort of the Persians", goes up in the east of the city.
    from: In 619, Alexandria [[ fell | sassanid-conquest-of-egypt ]] to the [[
      Sassanid Persians | sassanid-empire ]]. The city was mostly uninjured by
      the conquest and a new palace called //Tarawus// was erected in the
      eastern part of the city, later known as Qasr Faris, "fort of the
      Persians".[[ ^27 ]]
  - when: "629"
    what: The Byzantine emperor Heraclius recovers the city.
    from: Although the [[ Byzantine emperor | list-of-byzantine-emperors ]] [[
      Heraclius ]] recovered it in 629
  - when: AD 641
    what: The Arabs under 'Amr ibn al-'As take Alexandria after a 14-month siege,
      and the name is Arabicised.
    from: in 641 the Arabs under the general [[ 'Amr ibn al-'As ]] invaded it
      during the [[ Muslim conquest of Egypt ]], after a [[ siege |
      siege-of-alexandria-641 ]] that lasted 14 months.
  - when: 664–665
    what: Utba ibn Abi Sufyan builds a governor's palace in the city.
    from: The first Arab governor of Egypt recorded to have visited Alexandria was
      [[ Utba ibn Abi Sufyan ]], who strengthened the Arab presence and built
      a governor's palace in the city in 664–665.[[ ^28 ]][[ ^29 ]]
  - when: 1326 and 1349
    what: "Ibn Battuta visits the Pharos lighthouse twice: partly in ruins on the
      first visit, impossible to enter on the second."
    from: "Ibn Battuta also visited the Pharos lighthouse on two occasions: in
      1326 he found it to be partly in ruins and in 1349 it had deteriorated
      to the point that it was no longer possible to enter.[[ ^32 ]]"
  - when: 1477 AD (882 AH)
    what: The Mamluk Sultan Qa'it Bay establishes the Citadel of Qaitbay on the
      exact site of the Lighthouse.
    from: It was established in 1477 AD (882 [[ AH | hijri-year ]]) by the [[
      Mamluk | mamluk-sultanate ]] Sultan [[ Al-Ashraf Sayf al-Din Qa'it Bay |
      qaitbay ]]. The Citadel is located on the eastern side of the northern
      tip of Pharos Island at the mouth of the [[ Eastern Harbour |
      eastern-harbour-alexandria ]]. It was erected on the exact site of the
      famous [[ Lighthouse of Alexandria ]], which was one of the [[ Seven
      Wonders of the Ancient World ]].
  - when: "1517"
    what: After the Battle of Ridaniya the Ottoman Turks conquer the city; Ottoman
      rule lasts until 1798.
    from: After the [[ Battle of Ridaniya ]] in 1517, the city was conquered by
      the [[ Ottoman Turks ]] and remained under [[ Ottoman rule |
      egypt-eyalet ]] until 1798.
  - when: 2 July 1798
    what: French troops storm Alexandria during Napoleon's expedition to Egypt.
    from: Alexandria figured prominently in the military operations of [[ Napoleon
      ]]'s [[ expedition to Egypt | french-campaign-in-egypt-and-syria ]] in
      1798. French troops stormed the city on 2 July 1798, and it remained in
      their hands until the arrival of a British expedition in 1801.
  - when: 21 March 1801
    what: The British beat the French at the Battle of Alexandria; the city falls
      to them on 2 September 1801.
    from: The British won a considerable victory over the French at the [[ Battle
      of Alexandria | battle-of-alexandria-1801 ]] on 21 March 1801, following
      which they [[ besieged the city | siege-of-alexandria-1801 ]], which
      fell to them on 2 September 1801.
  - when: "1820"
    what: The Mahmoudiyah Canal is built and the city regains its former
      prominence.
    from: Alexandria lost much of its former importance to the Egyptian port city
      of [[ Rosetta ]] during the 9th to 18th centuries, and it only regained
      its former prominence with the construction of the [[ Mahmoudiyah Canal
      ]] in 1820.[[ ^38 ]]
  - when: "1860"
    what: The tramway network, the oldest in Africa, is built.
    from: An extensive tramway network was built in 1860 and is the oldest in
      Africa.[[ ^94 ]]
  - when: July 1882
    what: British naval forces bombard Alexandria and occupy it.
    from: In July 1882, the city came under [[ bombardment |
      bombardment-of-alexandria ]] from British naval forces and was
      occupied.[[ ^40 ]]
  - when: "1900"
    what: The catacombs of Kom El Shoqafa, long forgotten, are discovered by
      accident.
    from: The catacombs were long forgotten by the citizens until they were
      discovered by accident in 1900.[[ ^76 ]]
  - when: 26 October 1954
    what: A failed assassination attempt on Gamal Abdel Nasser in Alexandria's
      Mansheya Square.
    from: On 26 October 1954, Alexandria's Mansheya Square was the site of a
      failed assassination attempt on [[ Gamal Abdel Nasser ]].[[ ^41 ]]
  - when: "1956"
    what: Europeans begin leaving after the Suez Crisis; Nasser's nationalisation
      of property, at its height in 1961, drives out nearly all the rest.
    from: Europeans began leaving Alexandria following the 1956 [[ Suez Crisis ]]
      that led to an outburst of [[ Arab nationalism ]]. The nationalisation
      of property by Nasser, which reached its highest point in 1961, drove
      out nearly all the rest.[[ ^10 ]]
  - when: "2002"
    what: The Bibliotheca Alexandrina is inaugurated near the site of the old
      Library.
    from: The [[ Bibliotheca Alexandrina ]] was inaugurated in 2002, near the site
      of the old Library.[[ ^96 ]]
  - when: 2003-07-28
    what: Wikidata records Alexandria's heritage designation as a Tentative World
      Heritage Site from this date.
    from: "heritage-designation: value: Tentative World Heritage Site from:
      2003-07-28"
image-plan:
  - file: The_citadel_of_qaitbay_with_fishing_boats.jpg
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: The Citadel of Qaitbay seen from the Mediterranean. It was
      established in 1477 AD (882 AH) on the exact site of the Lighthouse of
      Alexandria.
  - file: Plan_of_Alexandria_c_30_BC_Otto_Puchstein_1890s_EN.svg
    role: full
    section: ancient-era
    caption: The city around 30 BC. Two colonnaded main streets, said to have been
      each about 60 m (200 ft) wide, crossed near the tomb of Alexander.
  - file: PSM_V78_D326_Ptolemy.png
    role: inline
    section: ancient-era
    caption: The Greco-Roman mathematician and astronomer Ptolemy lived and worked
      here, when Alexandria was a predominantly Greek city.
  - file: Macedonian_Army_Alexander.jpg
    role: inline
    section: ancient-layout
    caption: The Macedonian army, shown on the Alexander Sarcophagus. Alexander
      took the Egyptian Satrapy from the Persians before choosing this site
      for a Greek city of his own name.
  - file: PhareAlexandrie.jpg
    role: inline
    section: ancient-layout
    caption: The Lighthouse on second-century Alexandrian coins — reverses of
      coins of Antoninus Pius and Commodus. It was reputed to be 138 m (453
      ft) high and took 12 years to build.
  - file: Canopic_Street_Alexandria_1784_by_LF_Cassa.png
    role: inline
    section: ancient-layout
    caption: L. F. Cassas engraved the Canopic Street in 1784. The line of the
      great East–West "Canopic" street is still present in modern-day
      Alexandria.
  - file: S03_06_01_018_image_2380.jpg
    role: inline
    section: ancient-layout
    caption: One of the pair of obelisks known as Cleopatra's Needles, which stood
      by the Great Caesareum and were relocated to London and New York in the
      late 19th century.
  - file: Alexandria_by_Piri_Reis.jpg
    role: inline
    section: islamic-era
    caption: The Ottoman cartographer Piri Reis mapped Alexandria in the 16th
      century, in his Kitab-ı Bahriye (Book of Navigation).
  - file: Alexandrie_et_phare.jpg
    role: inline
    section: islamic-era
    caption: Alexandria in the late 18th century, by Luigi Mayer. The city had
      lost much of its importance to Rosetta, and would not regain it until
      the Mahmoudiyah Canal was built in 1820.
  - file: Louis-François_Cassas,_Alexandrie,_nommée_par_les_Arabes,_Eskanderyeh.jpg
    role: inline
    section: islamic-era
    caption: The city in the 1780s, mapped by Louis-François Cassas. French troops
      stormed it on 2 July 1798.
  - file: Bombardamento_Alessandria_1882.jpg
    role: inline
    section: islamic-era
    caption: British naval forces bombarded the city in July 1882, and occupied it.
  - file: Alex_21.JPG
    role: full
    section: climate-change
    caption: Alexandria's densely populated coastline. Its location on a Nile
      river delta makes it one of the most vulnerable cities to sea level rise
      in the entire world.
  - file: قصر_المنتزه_الحرملك_01.jpg
    role: inline
    section: montaza-palace
    caption: The Haramlik at Montaza, built by King Fuad I in 1925 as the summer
      residence of the royal family.
  - file: 113KOM_EL_SHOQAFA_CATACOMBS.jpg
    role: inline
    section: catacombs-of-kom-el-shoqafa
    caption: The multi-level labyrinth of Kom El Shoqafa, forgotten by the
      citizens until it was discovered by accident in 1900.
  - file: Mansheya-area-alexandria-6.jpg
    role: inline
    section: alexandria-naval-unknown-soldier-memorial
    caption: The memorial from behind. Built in 1933 to honour Khedive Ismail, it
      became the Monument to the Unknown Soldier in 1965 and his statue was
      removed.
  - file: Saint-Marc_Alexandria.jpg
    role: inline
    section: schools
    caption: Collège Saint Marc, one of the French schools in Alexandria run by
      Catholic missionaries.
  - file: Lycee_Francais_d'Alexandrie_2001.JPG
    role: inline
    section: schools
    caption: Lycée Français d'Alexandrie, the only school in the city that
      completely follows the French educational system.
  - file: Borg_El_Arab_International_Airport..jpg
    role: inline
    section: air
    caption: Alexandria International Airport, about 25 km (16 mi) from the city
      centre.
  - file: Alexandria_Corniche_(2346973947).jpg
    role: inline
    section: roads
    caption: The Corniche, about 17 km long, connects the Bahary area in the west
      to Mandara in the east.
  - file: Alexandria_red_double_decker_bus.jpg
    role: inline
    section: bus-system
    caption: The Alexandria Passenger Transport Authority was running 103 bus
      routes in July 2023.
  - file: Alexandria_tram.jpg
    role: inline
    section: trams
    caption: The tramway network was built in 1860 and is the oldest in Africa.
  - file: مكتبة_الإسكندرية_الجديدة.jpg
    role: full
    section: libraries
    caption: The Bibliotheca Alexandrina, inaugurated in 2002 near the site of the
      old Library.
  - file: Mezquita_abu_el_abbas-alejandria-2007.JPG
    role: inline
    section: islam
    caption: Abu al-Abbas al-Mursi Mosque in Bahary, the most famous mosque in
      Alexandria.
  - file: AlexMarkCathedralInside2.jpg
    role: inline
    section: christianity
    caption: Saint Mark Cathedral, the Patriarchal seat of the Pope of the Coptic
      Orthodox Church — though in practice the Patriarch has long resided in
      Cairo.
  - file: StCatherineChurchAlexandria.jpg
    role: inline
    section: christianity
    caption: Saint Catherine's Latin Catholic Cathedral. The Apostolic Vicariate
      seated here has jurisdiction over all Latin Catholics in Egypt.
  - file: Eliyahu-Hanavi-Synagogue.jpg
    role: inline
    section: judaism
    caption: The Eliyahu Hanavi Synagogue, the most important in Alexandria. The
      community once numbered 50,000 and is now estimated at below 50.
  - file: Borgelarabstadium2009.jpg
    role: inline
    section: sports
    caption: Borg El Arab Stadium, one of the city's four stadiums.
  - file: GD-EG-Alex-Stade002.JPG
    role: inline
    section: sports
    caption: Alexandria Stadium, built in 1929 and the oldest in Egypt. It holds
      20,000 people and was used for the 2006 African Cup of Nations.
glossary:
  - term: Rhacotis
    gloss: The Egyptian settlement Alexandria was established next to; it later
      became the city's Egyptian quarter.
    from: Alexandria was originally established near an [[ ancient Egyptian |
      ancient-egypt ]] settlement named [[ Rhacotis ]], which later became its
      Egyptian quarter.
  - term: Heptastadion
    gloss: The 1,260 m-long (4,130 ft) mole that joined the island of Pharos to
      the mainland — the name means "seven stadia".
    from: Alexandria consisted originally of little more than the island of
      Pharos, which was joined to the mainland by a 1,260 m-long (4,130 ft) [[
      mole | mole-architecture ]] and called the //Heptastadion// ("seven
      stadia"—a //[[ stadium ]]// was a Greek [[ unit of length ]] measuring
      approximately 180 m or 590 ft).
  - term: Stadium
    gloss: A Greek unit of length measuring approximately 180 m or 590 ft.
    from: a //[[ stadium ]]// was a Greek [[ unit of length ]] measuring
      approximately 180 m or 590 ft)
  - term: Brucheum
    gloss: The Royal or Greek quarter of the ancient city, and the most
      magnificent portion of it.
    from: Brucheum was the Royal or Greek quarter and formed the most magnificent
      portion of the city.
  - term: Musaeum (Museion)
    gloss: The temple of the Muses in the library complex; the modern English word
      "museum" derives from it.
    from: the temple of the [[ Muses | muse ]]—the [[ Museion | musaeum ]], Greek
      //Μουσείον// (from which the [[ Modern English ]] word //[[ museum |
      museum#etymology ]]// is derived).
  - term: Funduq
    gloss: An area, often fortified, within the city dedicated to the community of
      a trading nation under the authority of a consul.
    from: A funduq, in this context is an area, often fortified, within the city
      dedicated to the community of a trading nation under the authority of a
      consul.
  - term: Nazir al-khas
    gloss: The person in charge of managing the sultan's patrimony, to whom
      Alexandria's customs inspector answered directly.
    from: the sultan sent a customs inspector who answered directly to the //nazir
      al-khas// (person in charge of managing the sultan's patrimony).
  - term: Mazalim
    gloss: The sultan's courts, which tried Latin merchants in place of the qadis,
      the civil judges.
    from: Latin merchants could not be judged by the [[ qadis | qadi ]] (civil
      judges) but had to be judged by the [[ mazalim ]] (the sultan's
      courts).[[ ^36 ]]
further:
  - slug: alexander-the-great
    why: He founded the city in April 331 BC, chose the site for the two harbours
      a causeway to Pharos would make, and left Egypt a few months later,
      never returning to it in his life.
  - slug: nile-delta
    why: Alexandria sits at the delta's western edge, and that is also why it is
      one of the most vulnerable cities in the world to sea level rise.
  - slug: macedonia-ancient-kingdom
    why: The Macedonian army on the Alexander Sarcophagus is the one that took the
      Egyptian Satrapy from the Persians before the city existed.
anchors:
  hook: "[[ Cleopatra VII ]]'s royal quarters were inundated by earthquakes and
    tsunami, leading to gradual [[ subsidence ]] in the 4th century AD.[[ ^80
    ]] This underwater section, containing many of the most interesting
    sections of the Hellenistic city, including the palace quarter, has been
    explored since 1992 and is still being extensively investigated by the
    French underwater archaeologist [[ Franck Goddio ]] and his [[ team |
    institut-européen-darchéologie-sous-marine ]].[[ ^81 ]]"
  standfirst: Alexandria was originally established near an [[ ancient Egyptian
    | ancient-egypt ]] settlement named [[ Rhacotis ]], which later became its
    Egyptian quarter. The city was made the capital of the [[ Ptolemaic
    Kingdom ]] and became the foremost commercial, intellectual, and cultural
    centre for much of the [[ Hellenistic age ]] and [[ late antiquity ]];[[
    ^7 ]] at one time, it was the most populous city in the ancient world.
    Alexandria was best known for the [[ Lighthouse of Alexandria ]]
    (//Pharos//), one of the [[ Seven Wonders of the Ancient World ]]; its [[
    Great Library | library-of-alexandria ]], the largest in the ancient
    world; and the [[ Catacombs of Kom El Shoqafa ]], one of the [[ Seven
    Wonders of the Middle Ages | wonders-of-the-middle-ages ]].
